# Fixed-Point Control for AGI Safety  
## A Viability-Theoretic Framework for Constraining Stable Equilibria of Adaptive AI Systems  
### Version 2.0 — Mathematical Core

AGI safety may be approached by controlling the system’s reachable and stable fixed points, 
rather than attempting to control every individual action.

This is a sharper PhD question. We now develop its mathematical foundation.

---

## 1. Mathematical Preliminaries

### 1.1 State, Control, Dynamics

Let  
\[
\mathcal{X}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^n
\]
be the state space,  
\[
\mathcal{U}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^m
\]
the control set, and  
\[
\mathcal{W}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^p
\]
a disturbance/noise set. Consider the discrete-time controlled dynamical system

\[
x_{t+1}=F(x_t,u_t,w_t),\qquad
F:\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{U}\times\mathcal{W}\to\mathcal{X}.
\]

In the deterministic case we write

\[
x_{t+1}=F(x_t,u_t).
\]

Continuous-time analogues are written \(\dot x=f(x,u)\). Stochastic systems are represented by a transition kernel \(P(dx'\mid x,u)\).

### 1.2 Fixed Points, Invariant Sets, and Attractors

For an autonomous map \(F:\mathcal{X}\to\mathcal{X}\), a **fixed point** is \(x^\*\in\mathcal{X}\) such that

\[
F(x^\*)=x^\*.
\]

For a controlled system, a **controlled fixed point** is a pair \((x^\*,u^\*)\) such that

\[
F(x^\*,u^\*)=x^\*.
\]

The **fixed-point set** is

\[
\operatorname{Fix}(F)
=
\{x\in\mathcal{X}:\exists u\in\mathcal{U},\;F(x,u)=x\}.
\]

A set \(\mathcal{A}\subseteq\mathcal{X}\) is **invariant** if \(F(\mathcal{A})\subseteq\mathcal{A}\). It is an **attractor** if it is compact, invariant, and there exists an open neighborhood \(\mathcal{N}\) of \(\mathcal{A}\) such that

\[
\lim_{t\to\infty}\operatorname{dist}(x_t,\mathcal{A})=0
\quad\text{for all }x_0\in\mathcal{N}.
\]

The **basin of attraction** of \(\mathcal{A}\) is

\[
\mathcal{B}(\mathcal{A})
=
\left\{
x_0\in\mathcal{X}:
\lim_{t\to\infty}\operatorname{dist}(x_t,\mathcal{A})=0
\right\}.
\]

### 1.3 Viability and Reachability

Let  
\[
\mathcal{V}\subseteq\mathcal{X}
\]
be the **viable set** (safe states), e.g.

\[
\mathcal{V}=\{x\in\mathcal{X}:S(x)\le S_{\max}\}.
\]

Let  
\[
\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{adm}}\subseteq\mathcal{U}
\]
be the set of admissible controls.

The **reachable set** from \(x_0\) while remaining in \(\mathcal{V}\) is

\[
\mathcal{R}_{\mathcal{V}}(x_0)
=
\left\{
x\in\mathcal{V}:
\exists T\ge 0,\;\exists u_{0:T-1}\in\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{adm}},
\;x_T=x,\;x_t\in\mathcal{V}\;\forall t\in\{0,\dots,T\}
\right\}.
\]

The **viability kernel** of \(\mathcal{V}\) is

\[
\operatorname{Viab}_{\mathcal{V}}
=
\left\{
x\in\mathcal{V}:
\exists u_0,u_1,\dots\in\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{adm}}
\text{ such that }x_t\in\mathcal{V}\;\forall t\ge 0
\right\}.
\]

The **viable-reachable set** to a target \(x^\*\) is

\[
\mathcal{R}_{\mathcal{V}}(x^\*)
=
\left\{
x_0\in\mathcal{V}:x^\*\in\mathcal{R}_{\mathcal{V}}(x_0)
\right\}.
\]

This yields the **Accessibility Principle**:

\[
\boxed{
\text{Existence}
\;\rightarrow\;
\text{Accessibilité viable}
\;\rightarrow\;
\text{Atteignabilité}
\;\rightarrow\;
\text{Stabilité}
}
\]

and the operational condition

\[
\boxed{
x^\*\in\mathcal{E}\cap\mathcal{V}\cap\mathcal{R}_{\mathcal{V}}(x_0)
}
\]

where \(\mathcal{E}\) is the set of equilibria.

