## 2. The Safety Problem

Partition the fixed points into safe and unsafe:

\[
\operatorname{Fix}(F)
=
\mathcal{F}_{\text{safe}}
\cup
\mathcal{F}_{\text{unsafe}}.
\]

The safety objective is not necessarily to eliminate every unsafe fixed point. Instead:

\[
\boxed{
\mathcal{F}_{\text{unsafe}}
\cap
\mathcal{R}_{\mathcal{V}}(x_0)
=
\varnothing
}
\]

while ensuring that at least one safe equilibrium remains reachable:

\[
\boxed{
\mathcal{F}_{\text{safe}}
\cap
\mathcal{R}_{\mathcal{V}}(x_0)
\neq\varnothing.
}
\]

A stronger formulation uses the **reachable fixed-point set**:

\[
\mathcal{F}_{\mathcal{R}}(x_0)
=
\operatorname{Fix}(F)
\cap
\mathcal{R}_{\mathcal{V}}(x_0).
\]

Then the safety condition becomes

\[
\boxed{
\mathcal{F}_{\mathcal{R}}(x_0)
\subseteq
\mathcal{F}_{\text{safe}}
}
\]

or, more realistically,

\[
\boxed{
\mathcal{F}_{\mathcal{R}}(x_0)
\cap
\mathcal{F}_{\text{unsafe}}
=
\varnothing.
}
\]

This avoids claiming that dangerous equilibria must literally not exist. They may exist mathematically. The safety requirement is that **the system cannot reach them under admissible dynamics**.

We therefore formulate:

\[
\boxed{
\text{AGI Risk}
\approx
\text{Reachable Dangerous Attractors}
}
\]

not simply

\[
\text{AGI Risk}\approx\text{Capability}.
\]

