## 3. Control Synthesis

### 3.1 Control Lyapunov and Control Barrier Functions

To simultaneously guarantee convergence to a safe fixed point \(x^\*\) and preserve viability, we use a **Control Lyapunov Function (CLF)** and a **Control Barrier Function (CBF)**.

A CLF is a function \(V:\mathcal{X}\to\mathbb{R}_{\ge 0}\) with

\[
V(x^\*)=0,\qquad V(x)>0\quad\text{for }x\neq x^\*,
\]

and

\[
V(F(x,u))-V(x)\le -\alpha(V(x))
\]

for some class-\(\mathcal{K}\) function \(\alpha\).

A CBF is a function \(B:\mathcal{X}\to\mathbb{R}\) defining the safe set

\[
\mathcal{C}=\{x\in\mathcal{X}:B(x)\ge 0\}\subseteq\mathcal{V},
\]

with

\[
B(F(x,u))-B(x)\ge -\gamma(B(x))
\]

for some extended class-\(\mathcal{K}\) function \(\gamma\).

The control is obtained by solving the quadratic program

\[
\begin{aligned}
\min_{u\in\mathcal{U}}\quad & \|u-u_{\mathrm{ref}}\|^2 \\
\text{s.t.}\quad
& V(F(x,u))-V(x)\le -\alpha(V(x)), \\
& B(F(x,u))-B(x)\ge -\gamma(B(x)).
\end{aligned}
\]

Feasibility of this program implies the existence of a viable trajectory converging to \(x^\*\).

### 3.2 Hamilton–Jacobi Reachability

For exact computation, define the value function

\[
V(x)
=
\min_{u\in\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{adm}}}
\max_{t\ge 0}
c(x_t,u_t).
\]

The viability kernel is the sublevel set

\[
\operatorname{Viab}_{\mathcal{V}}
=
\{x\in\mathcal{X}:V(x)\le 0\}.
\]

In continuous time, \(V\) satisfies the Hamilton–Jacobi–Isaacs equation

\[
0
=
\min_{u\in\mathcal{U}}
\max\left\{
c(x,u)+\nabla V(x)\cdot f(x,u),\;-V(x)
\right\}.
\]

In discrete time,

\[
V(x)
=
\min_{u\in\mathcal{U}}
\max\left\{
c(x,u)+V(F(x,u)),\;-V(x)
\right\}.
\]

Neural network approximations make this tractable in high dimensions.

### 3.3 Model Predictive Control

At each step, solve

\[
\begin{aligned}
\min_{u_{0:N-1}}\quad
& \sum_{k=0}^{N-1}c(x_k,u_k)+V_f(x_N) \\
\text{s.t.}\quad
& x_{k+1}=F(x_k,u_k), \\
& x_k\in\mathcal{V},\qquad u_k\in\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{adm}}, \\
& x_N\in\mathcal{X}_f\subseteq\mathcal{V},\qquad x^\*\in\mathcal{X}_f.
\end{aligned}
\]

The feasible region of this problem is precisely the viable-reachable set \(\mathcal{R}_{\mathcal{V}}(x^\*)\). Receding-horizon implementation constructs a viable trajectory to \(x^\*\) whenever one exists.

### 3.4 Constrained Learning and Attractor Modification

When the dynamics \(F_\theta\) depend on parameters \(\theta\), we can shape the attractor landscape by solving

\[
\theta^\*
=
\arg\min_\theta
\;
\mathcal{L}_{\text{task}}(\theta)
+
\lambda\,
\mathcal{L}_{\text{safety}}(\theta),
\]

where \(\mathcal{L}_{\text{safety}}\) penalizes unsafe attractors or enlarges the basin of safe attractors. CBF constraints can be embedded directly into policy optimization.

