## 6. Theorem Targets

### Theorem 1 — Safe Attractor Exclusion

Assume:

1. \(\mathcal{X},\mathcal{U}\) compact; \(F\) continuous.
2. \(\mathcal{V}\) closed; \(x_0\in\operatorname{Viab}_{\mathcal{V}}\).
3. \(\mathcal{F}_{\text{unsafe}}\cap\mathcal{R}_{\mathcal{V}}(x_0)=\varnothing\).
4. Controllability: for all \(x\in\operatorname{Viab}_{\mathcal{V}}\), there exists \(u\) steering to \(\operatorname{Viab}_{\mathcal{V}}\).

Then there exists a controller \(u(t)\) such that

\[
x(t)\in\mathcal{V}\quad\forall t\ge 0,
\qquad
\operatorname{dist}(x_t,\mathcal{A}_{\text{safe}})\to 0.
\]

### Theorem 2 — Viability-Preserving Convergence

If there exist a CLF \(V\) and a CBF \(B\) satisfying the conditions of Section 3.1, then the quadratic program is feasible and the closed-loop system converges to \(x^\*\) while remaining in \(\mathcal{V}\).

### Theorem 3 — Partial Observability

Under detectability and finite ambiguity, unsafe attractors can be excluded with probability at least \(1-\delta\) from finite observations.

